Config { font = "-*-Fixed-Bold-R-Normal-*-13-*-*-*-*-*-*-*"
       , bgColor = "black"
       , fgColor = "grey"
       -- Deliberately NOT `OnScreen n`: this one config is shared by TWO xmobar
       -- processes, one per monitor, and each is pointed at its screen with `-x N`
       -- on the command line in ~/.xmonad/xmonad.hs. Hardcoding OnScreen here would
       -- fight the flag. `Top` means "top of whichever screen -x selected".
       -- 2026-08-07: briefly was `OnScreen 1 Top` (single bar on the vertical
       -- monitor) before Ben clarified he wanted a bar on both.
       , position = Top
       , commands =
         [ Run Date "%a %b %_d %l:%M" "date" 10
         -- Locks prints CAPS / NUM / SCROLL only while that lock is ON, and nothing
         -- otherwise, so the bar stays quiet until it has something to say.
         , Run Locks
         -- Battery, 2026-08-28 (carbon task 3). A Com script rather than xmobar's
         -- own BatteryP plugin, for one reason: this config is shared by every
         -- machine and beast-arch has no battery. xmobar's config is data with no
         -- conditionals in it, so there is nowhere HERE to write "laptop only",
         -- and BatteryP on a batteryless box prints its own error text into the
         -- bar. ~/bin/battery-xmobar prints NOTHING when it finds no battery, so
         -- the %battery% slot collapses and beast-arch's bar is unchanged.
         --
         -- Absolute path on purpose: xmobar is started by xmonad, which is started
         -- by .xinitrc, which never sources .zshrc -- ~/bin is not reliably on PATH
         -- at that point, and a Com whose command is not found fails silently.
         --
         -- 100 = 10 seconds. Battery state does not move faster than that, and the
         -- script reads three files under /sys per tick.
         , Run Com "/home/ben/bin/battery-xmobar" [] "battery" 100
         , Run StdinReader
         -- What dunst is holding back, 2026-09-02 (task 58). ALWAYS prints --
         -- "Notifications: On" / "Off" / "Off (N held)" -- and the note by the
         -- template below says why: Run Com retains its previous value when a
         -- command outputs nothing. (Corrected 2026-09-08: this comment used to
         -- say it "prints nothing unless dunst is paused", which is the behaviour
         -- that had to be REMOVED to stop the field sticking.)
         -- ABSOLUTE path on purpose: xmobar is spawned by
         -- xmonad from .xinitrc, which does not source .zshrc, so ~/.local/bin
         -- is not reliably on PATH there (.xinitrc says so itself at line 130).
         -- Polls every 5s; the script exits early unless dunst is running.
         , Run Com "/home/ben/.local/bin/dunst-waiting" [] "dunstwait" 20
         -- Pending agent messages, 2026-09-08 (beast-arch task 59). Absolute path
         -- for the same reason as dunstwait above.
         --
         -- `inbox count` ALWAYS prints a line, including "Inbox: 0" -- it must,
         -- because Run Com retains its previous value when a command outputs
         -- nothing, which is the bug that made the dunst field stick. That is also
         -- why this reads as words rather than a glyph: a count of zero has to be
         -- distinguishable from a field that has stopped updating.
         --
         -- Verified under `env -i PATH=/usr/bin:/bin HOME=/home/ben`, which is much
         -- closer to what xmobar actually gets than a login shell is. `count --all`
         -- shells out to nothing -- no git, no herdr -- so it has no PATH needs
         -- beyond python3 itself.
         --
         -- 300s, not 20: the SessionStart hook is the primary channel and fires on
         -- /clear, compact and resume as well as startup. This field is Ben's
         -- escalation for messages no agent has picked up, so it is not urgent to
         -- the second.
         , Run Com "/home/ben/projects/station-maintenance/bin/inbox" ["count","--all"] "inbox" 300
         ]
       , sepChar = "%"
       , alignSep = "}{"
       -- %locks% goes in the CENTRE section (between } and {), deliberately.
       -- Putting it in the right section next to the clock would push the clock
       -- leftwards every time CAPS appears, into the stalonetray icons that sit
       -- at x 1660-1788 on the LEFT monitor. The centre is empty, fixed, and hard
       -- to miss.
       --
       -- Note this config now drives two bars of DIFFERENT widths -- 1920px on
       -- HDMI-A-0, 1080px on the vertical HDMI-A-1. The tray only overlaps the
       -- left one. Anything tuned by pixel offset here is tuned for the wide bar
       -- and is tighter on the narrow one; `shorten 50` on ppTitle is the most
       -- likely thing to look cramped on the vertical monitor.
       -- MERGED 2026-09-07. The two fields below arrived from opposite machines and
       -- occupy DIFFERENT sections, so both are kept: %battery% in the centre next to
       -- %locks% (carbon task 3), %dunstwait% in the right ahead of the tray padding
       -- (beast-arch task 57). Neither note supersedes the other; read both before
       -- moving anything, because each records a collision already walked into once.
       --
       -- MERGED AGAIN 2026-09-08, same shape, third field. %inbox% (beast-arch task
       -- 59) and %battery% were added on opposite machines against the same parent,
       -- so git conflicted on this one template line and nowhere else -- the two
       -- Run Com blocks above merged clean. Both kept, again: %battery% centre,
       -- %inbox% LEFTMOST in the right section, ahead of %dunstwait%.
       --
       -- Leftmost is the safe end. The right section is right-aligned and grows
       -- leftwards from 1920, so the field furthest left is the one furthest from
       -- the tray at x 1660-1788 -- the collision both notes below record. A field
       -- added at the RIGHT end of this section is the one to think twice about.
       --
       -- %battery% is in the CENTRE section, with %locks%, and that is not a style
       -- choice. It was in the right section next to the clock for one revision and
       -- the tray ate it: stalonetray is a dock at x 1660-1788 drawn ON TOP of the
       -- bar (window_layer top), the right section is right-aligned and grows
       -- leftwards from 1920, and "BAT 98%" landed straight underneath the icons.
       -- All that showed on screen was the trailing "%". Exactly the collision the
       -- note above warns about, walked into from the other direction.
       --
       -- The centre is empty and fixed, which is why %locks% is here too.
       -- %battery% carries its own <fc> markup, including the white-on-red block
       -- below 20%, so it is deliberately NOT wrapped in a colour here. On a
       -- machine with no battery it expands to nothing and the centre section is
       -- what it always was, one leading space aside.
       --
       -- %dunstwait% sits in the RIGHT section, deliberately padded LEFT OF THE
       -- TRAY (Ben's call, 2026-09-05). It reads "Notifications: On" /
       -- "Notifications: Off" / "Notifications: Off (N held)"; it is never
       -- empty, because xmobar's Run Com retains its previous value when a
       -- command outputs nothing, which is what made the field stick.
       --
       -- ★ The run of spaces before %date% is LOAD-BEARING, not formatting. ★
       -- The right section is right-aligned at the bar's edge, so the padding is
       -- what pushes this field left of stalonetray's icons instead of under
       -- them.
       --
       -- ⚠️ These numbers are MEASURED, not derived -- a first attempt computed
       -- them from assumed metrics and landed the text under the tray. Method:
       -- capture the bar with `ffmpeg -f x11grab -video_size 1920x18 -i :0.0+0,0`
       -- then find the ink columns (ignore rows 14+, which pick up the border of
       -- the window below and span the full width). Tray geometry comes from
       -- `xdotool search --class stalonetray getwindowgeometry`.
       --
       -- ★ A SPACE IS NOT A CHARACTER WIDTH HERE. ★ The font renders glyphs at
       -- ~6.9px but spaces at ~4.0px, so scaling the padding by the character
       -- width undershoots. Two attempts missed for exactly this reason before
       -- it was measured bycomparing captures.
       --
       -- Measured 2026-09-05 on the LEFT monitor:
       --     stalonetray window        ->  x1660-1788 (128x16 at 1660,1)
       --     clock, 110px / 16 chars   ->  x1809-1918, i.e. 6.9px per GLYPH
       --     22 spaces -> text began x1594 ; 34 spaces -> x1546
       --         => 48px for 12 spaces = 4.0px per SPACE
       --     at 34 spaces the text ran x1546-~1663, still ~3px under the tray
       -- 43 spaces shifts it a further ~36px left: right edge ~x1627, clearing
       -- the tray by ~33px.
       --
       -- Because the section is right-aligned, longer text ("Off (3 held)")
       -- grows LEFTWARD, away from the tray, so the clearance holds for every
       -- state -- the right edge is what the padding fixes.
       --
       -- Delete or shorten that padding and the field lands underneath the tray
       -- icons. If the tray is ever resized or moved, re-derive the count; the
       -- old note about %locks% shoving the clock applies here too.
       --
       -- On the 1080px vertical monitor the tray does not overlap at all, so the
       -- padding is merely cosmetic there -- but it does eat 154px of a narrower
       -- bar, which is the "tuned for the wide bar" caveat noted above.
       , template = "%StdinReader% }%battery% <fc=#ff5555>%locks%</fc>{ <fc=#50fa7b>%inbox%</fc>  <fc=#bd93f9>%dunstwait%</fc>                                           <fc=#ee9a00>%date%</fc>"
       }
       -- wishlist
       -- 1) clickable workspaces
